Init scripts for slinit. Each executable script here takes start, stop,
restart and status, and carries an LSB header:

### BEGIN INIT INFO
# Provides:          name
# Required-Start:    other services it needs
# Required-Stop:
# Should-Start:      services to start first when present
# Default-Start:     2 3 4 5
# Default-Stop:      0 1 6
# Short-Description: what it is
# X-Slate-Exec:      /path/to/daemon --foreground   (optional: slinit runs
#                    and supervises it, restarting with backoff)
### END INIT INFO

slinit starts and stops scripts in the order these headers imply.
`service NAME start|stop|restart|status` asks slinit to do so by hand.
Slate ships eight services: sshd, its own SSH server (ADR 0030),
slate-brokerd, the privilege broker (ADR 0032), devd, the device
database, seatd, the seat daemon (ADR 0033), netd, the network daemon
(ADR 0048), logd, the system log (ADR 0060), slate-btd, Bluetooth
(ADR 0113), and fstrim, the weekly trim (ADR 0122).

A service's output (and that of everything it starts) goes to
/var/log/services/NAME.log; `service NAME log` shows it. Its processes
carry the service's name (/proc/PID/status `Service:`), so stopping it
stops all of them.
